<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Addizione Erculea</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1492">1492</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Herculean Addition</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Addizione Erculea was a planned extension of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a> begun in 1492 for Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ercole-i-d-este">Ercole I d'Este</a> and designed by the architect Biagio Rossetti. It roughly doubled the city's area with broad straight streets, new walls and palaces such as the Palazzo dei Diamanti, and is often called the first modern city plan in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. It is part of Ferrara's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> World Heritage listing.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/addizione-erculea.jpg" alt="Addizione Erculea" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pallares1 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ferrara_diamanti3.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The walled medieval city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a> could not grow southward, where it was hemmed in by a branch of the Po delta. In 1450 the previous duke, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borso-deste">Borso d'Este</a>, had enlarged it slightly to the south with land reclaimed from the river banks. Ercole d'Este, however, had suffered attacks and sieges from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> to the north and the Papal States to the south. In 1492, to make room for the growing city and to create a stronger fortress able to withstand sieges, he commissioned the architect Biagio Rossetti to plan an expansion to the north.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The old northern walls, which met the north flank of the Castello Estense, were razed and their moat, the present Corso Giovecca, was filled in. In place of the dense and haphazard streets of the medieval centre, the new quarter was laid out on two main axes, analogous to a decumanus maximus and a cardo maximus: an east-west road, now Corso Porta Po, Corso Biagio Rossetti and Corso Porta Mare, and a north-south street, now Corso Ercole I d'Este, running from the castle.</p><p>The addition left room for cultivated land and private parks, areas now taken by the Cemetery of the Certosa and the Jewish cemetery. Over the following decades and centuries the quarter received the Palazzina di Marfisa d'Este, the church of Santa Chiara, the Palazzo Roverella, the Palazzo Prosperi-Sacrati, the church of the Teatini, the church of San Carlo and the 17th-century Teatro Comunale.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The Addizione Erculea is held up as an example of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> urban planning. The walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a> once ran for 13 kilometres, and some 9 kilometres of them still stand, mostly to the north and east of the city. The Porta degli Angeli was an original northern gate of the addition.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Addizione Erculea” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ercole I's planned extension of the city, the <b>Addizione Erculea</b>, was one of the first modern urban plans.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-ferrara">Duchy of Ferrara</a></li></ul></section>
